---
import Prose from './Prose.astro';
import CodeBlock from './CodeBlock.astro';
import Callout from './Callout.astro';
import { SKILLS, SKILL_FRONTMATTER, TECHCART_RULES } from '../data/guide';

const rulesBlock = `# CLAUDE.md — rules\n\n${TECHCART_RULES.map((r) => `- ${r.replace(/`/g, '')}`).join('\n')}`;
---

<Prose class="max-w-none">
  <p>
    TechCart is built with Claude Code. The repository carries two kinds of durable instruction that
    the model reads on every session: skills, which are packaged workflows, and rules, which are
    always-on project context.
  </p>

  <h3>Skills</h3>
  <p>
    A skill is a packaged set of instructions for a recurring task. It is invoked by name or as a
    slash command, and the model loads the instructions and follows them in place of its default
    approach. Each skill is a folder under .claude/skills/ that contains a SKILL.md file.
  </p>
  <p>
    SKILL.md is YAML frontmatter plus a body. The frontmatter has a name and a description whose
    wording carries the trigger phrases the model matches against; an optional allowed-tools list
    narrows what the skill may call. The body is the instruction set, and supporting files can sit
    alongside it in the folder.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock title="SKILL.md" code={SKILL_FRONTMATTER} />

  <p>The TechCart repository ships four skills:</p>
  <ul>
    {SKILLS.map((skill) => (
      <li>
        <code>{skill.name}</code> &mdash; {skill.purpose}
      </li>
    ))}
  </ul>
  <p>
    To create one, run the skill-creator skill, describe the task and its trigger phrases, and keep
    the instruction body short and imperative. Reach for a skill over a plain prompt when a task is
    repeated, multi-step, and has a fixed correct sequence &mdash; release steps, review checklists,
    repo-specific flows.
  </p>

  <h3>Rules</h3>
  <p>
    CLAUDE.md at the repository root holds always-on context: what the project is, its invariants,
    its conventions. Keep it short and factual. A scoped CLAUDE.md in a subdirectory such as
    backend/CLAUDE.md overrides the root for that subtree &mdash; the most-specific file wins, so
    workspace-specific rules belong there. AGENTS.md is the cross-tool equivalent, and TechCart
    keeps both at the root and per workspace.
  </p>

  <Callout type="note">
    <code>.claude/settings.json</code> holds tool permissions and hooks. Any &ldquo;whenever X
    happens, do Y&rdquo; automation is a hook &mdash; the harness runs it, not the model &mdash; so
    it cannot be written as a prose rule.
  </Callout>

  <p>Good rules are:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>imperative &mdash; &ldquo;Do X&rdquo;, not &ldquo;the project prefers X&rdquo;;</li>
    <li>one per line, each independently verifiable;</li>
    <li>shown by example rather than described;</li>
    <li>deleted the moment they go stale.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>A representative rule block for TechCart:</p>
  <CodeBlock title="CLAUDE.md" code={rulesBlock} />
</Prose>
